 Arrays store list of items (objects & primitives)
 these items store sequentially in memory.
 for example we want to store integer (we know integer is 4 bytes)

 imagine the address of first item on memory is 100
 the second item will store on                  104
 the third item will store on                   108


Note: that's the reason we can find easily each item "O(1)"
      because the address of each item is predictable
      (the address of 20th item is 180!)

Note: that's the reason in arrays
      we just can store one type!
      each item should have same size!

Note: Arrays in JAVA is "STATIC"
      it means when we are allocate them, we should specify their size
      (it means they have not dynamic size on RAM, the size is static, prefix)
      (in python Arrays are dynamic!)
      so in bad scenario, when we are inserting, the array has not enough size
      he has to copy all element on another bigger array
      so inserting is O(n)

Note: if we want to delete last item, it just O(1)
      but in bad scenario, if we want to delete first item
      whole list should shift left!
      so deleting id O(n)

Note: when our list size change rapidly,
      we should use LINK-LIST

LOOKUP --> O(1)
insert --> O(n)
delete --> O(n)